                  <provenance type="#b"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_183">Dunfermline, Fife.
                     Benedictine abbey of the Holy Trinity</orgName><p>This attribution was rejected
                     in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964). The
                     corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card recorded the
                     following evidence, at that stage with some skepticism:</p> <p>'Fol. 203:
                     Protest by Adam Forman, prior of Dunfermline, against a sentence of
                     excommunication, 3 May 1525. Transcript of lost original written at Pluscarden
                     (cell of Dunfermline) for Richard Bothwell, abobt of Dunfermline. Skene
                     apparently attributes the manuscript to Dunfermline purely on the strength of
                     the document above.'</p> <p>However, the Dunfermline attribution was later
                     accepted in the Medieval Libraries of Great Britain Supplement (1987). MLGB3
                     (2010-2015) clarified that this attribution was listed thanks to the advice of
                     John Durkan, and noted: 'the binding leaves (not mentioned in Summary Catalogue
                     no. 3888) are from Dunfermline abbey accounts.'</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">'I Spend luffe' at fol. 80: one of this name
                     was a monk of Dunfermline, s. xv ex.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Family of Drummond of Hawthornden. <q>Mr
                     James Drummond 1650. Gifted theis book to Coronall Fairfax the 17 of December,
                     Anno 1650.</q></provenance>
               </history>
